WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.005
The festive sale is six weeks away.

2
00:00:03.025 --> 00:00:05.330
The order pipeline must survive an outage,

3
00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:08.515
keep payments correct, and stop bad files.

4
00:00:08.535 --> 00:00:09.940
Four ideas get you there.

5
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.715
Let's go.

6
00:00:11.735 --> 00:00:14.200
First: which tool cleans the data?

7
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.300
Here is the trick.

8
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.985
Pick the tool for the people who will run it.

9
00:00:18.005 --> 00:00:21.023
A team that knows SQL, with data already in BigQuery?

10
00:00:21.043 --> 00:00:22.420
Dataform.

11
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.032
A team that wants drag and drop, with no code?

12
00:00:25.052 --> 00:00:26.905
Cloud Data Fusion.

13
00:00:26.925 --> 00:00:29.517
Engineers with a fast stream and custom logic?

14
00:00:29.537 --> 00:00:30.915
Dataflow.

15
00:00:30.935 --> 00:00:34.650
And if you already have Spark jobs, keep them, on Dataproc.

16
00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:39.525
The trap: a no-code tool for a high volume, low latency stream.

17
00:00:39.545 --> 00:00:42.185
Second: what happens when something breaks?

18
00:00:42.205 --> 00:00:43.935
Write down two numbers first.

19
00:00:43.955 --> 00:00:46.795
RPO: how much data you can lose.

20
00:00:46.815 --> 00:00:49.505
And RTO: how long you can be down.

21
00:00:49.525 --> 00:00:51.918
Then buy the cheapest design that meets both.

22
00:00:51.938 --> 00:00:53.590
Not the fanciest.

23
00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:54.840
For Cloud Storage,

24
00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:59.975
a dual-region bucket with turbo replication gives an RPO of 15 minutes.

25
00:00:59.995 --> 00:01:01.800
BigQuery has time travel.

26
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:05.360
You can read a table as it was, up to 7 days ago.

27
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:09.235
Cloud SQL high availability keeps a standby in another zone.

28
00:01:09.255 --> 00:01:11.170
It takes over on its own.

29
00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:12.945
But a read replica does not.

30
00:01:12.965 --> 00:01:15.570
You must promote it by hand.

31
00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.855
Third: keeping payments correct.

32
00:01:17.875 --> 00:01:19.742
Moving money touches two rows.

33
00:01:19.762 --> 00:01:21.505
Both must change, or neither.

34
00:01:21.525 --> 00:01:23.340
That is a transaction.

35
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.250
Spanner does this across rows, tables, and even regions.

36
00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:30.935
Cloud SQL and AlloyDB do it inside one region.

37
00:01:30.955 --> 00:01:33.348
Bigtable is atomic for one row only.

38
00:01:33.368 --> 00:01:35.545
So never use it to move money.

39
00:01:35.565 --> 00:01:38.120
Stronger and wider guarantees cost more.

40
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.070
Choose the weakest one that is enough.

41
00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:43.305
Fourth: stopping bad data.

42
00:01:43.325 --> 00:01:45.990
Check the data in the place where it changes.

43
00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:47.702
Dataform has assertions.

44
00:01:47.722 --> 00:01:50.450
Dataplex has data quality scans.

45
00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:55.560
After a migration, the Data Validation Tool compares the source with the target.

46
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.685
In Dataflow, send bad rows to a dead-letter table.

47
00:01:58.705 --> 00:02:01.735
Do not drop them, and do not crash.

48
00:02:01.755 --> 00:02:03.220
Quick recap.

49
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.707
Pick the tool for the team.

50
00:02:04.727 --> 00:02:07.555
Write down RPO and RTO first.

51
00:02:07.575 --> 00:02:09.880
Choose the weakest guarantee that works.

52
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.365
And validate where the data changes.

53
00:02:12.385 --> 00:02:15.385
Now try the checkpoint below.
